{# src/dbml_sharepoint/templates/deploy/_reader_enrolment.js.j2
   Phase body: enrol the ONE account named by `build --enterprise-reader`
   into the mapping's `enroll_enterprise_reader` group, which holds Read.
   Emitted only when that flag was given, so an ordinary build carries no
   enrolment code at all. Unlike the operator's run-scoped enrolment, this
   membership is PERMANENT once the run reaches the end. If a later phase
   aborts, deploy.js.j2's finally removes the account this phase just
   enrolled -- a rollback of this run's own write, not a general reconciler
   for membership some earlier run may have left behind.
   Step 0 checks what the group GRANTS, by reading the live BasePermissions
   of every level it is assigned rather than trusting a level named Read to
   be the built-in one. Step 1 checks what it ALREADY HOLDS, because a
   binding this bundle did not create is inherited on enrolment just the
   same. The rest guard WHO is enrolled: every resolution is refused unless
   it is a single user (PrincipalType strictly 1), does not match one of the
   three tenant-wide-claim needles at step 4, and matches the address the
   build asked for; the group must hold nobody but that account already
   (step 7); and the membership is then read back before the run is allowed
   to call it done.

   Those needles are NOT full coverage of the tenant-wide claims: they
   cover two of the four Learn names. Read the dated KNOWN LIMIT at step 4
   before treating this as a closed door -- it records which two, why the
   other two are deliberately not guessed, and which of the guards here
   the residual risk actually rests on. #}
{% if enterprise_reader %}
  // === Enterprise reader enrolment (--enterprise-reader) ===
  // Grants one named service account read-only access to the registers
  // this bundle provisions, by adding it to the group the mapping flagged
  // with `enroll_enterprise_reader`, a group the validator requires to
  // hold Read and nothing more. The group's grants are unchanged here;
  // this phase decides WHO is in it, and refuses to put anybody in it if
  // the level behind the grant does not actually read (step 0) or if the
  // group already holds a binding stronger than reading (step 1).
  //
  // THIS MEMBERSHIP IS PERMANENT ONLY ONCE THE RUN REACHES THE END. Operator
  // self-enrolment (the phase before this one) always removes itself on the
  // way out, because it exists only to let this run write. A reporting
  // account is meant to exist between runs, but a run that aborts after
  // this phase has deploy.js.j2's finally remove the account it just
  // enrolled, same as any other write a run cannot leave half done. Once
  // the run reaches the end, nothing removes it; remove it by hand in Site
  // permissions > Groups.
  //
  // The group must hold nobody but the named account. If it already holds
  // anyone else, this phase ABORTS the run before adding anything and
  // removes nobody: a second reader is a permanent grant nothing here can
  // safely undo, so it goes to a human.
  //
  // Everything below is a guard against granting Read to the wrong
  // principal, which is the failure this phase can cause and cannot show:
  // the deploy reads back byte-identical whether the member is one service
  // account or the entire tenant. `ensureuser` will happily resolve a
  // security group, a distribution list, or an everyone-claim, so each is
  // refused explicitly and the run aborts before any list is created.
{% if phase.first_in_group %}
  log('INFO', 'Group {{ phase.group_number }}: {{ phase.group_name }}');
{% endif %}
  log('INFO', 'Starting Phase {{ phase.number }}: {{ phase.name }}.');
  // Declared outside the block below so the abort at the end of this phase
  // can read it. See that abort for why a local count and not
  // `summary.errors.length`.
  let readerEnrolmentErrors = 0;
  {
    const READER_ADDRESS = {{ enterprise_reader | tojson }};
    // The build refuses --enterprise-reader against a mapping that declares
    // no flagged group, and the validator refuses more than one, so on any
    // bundle this tool produced the list holds exactly one group. Written
    // as a loop anyway: a hand-edited bundle then reports per group instead
    // of silently enrolling into whichever one happened to come first.
    const readerGroups = SCHEMA.groups.filter(g => g.enroll_enterprise_reader);
    // The bitmap a level must carry to be worth enrolling into, and the two
    // bits whose absence only narrows it. Emitted from
    // analysis/permissions.py so the values this checks against and the
    // values a declared level is BUILT from have one definition.
    const READER_REQUIRED_BITS = {{ enterprise_reader_required_bits | tojson }};
    const READER_ADVISORY_BITS = {{ enterprise_reader_advisory_bits | tojson }};
    // The bits that make a binding the group ALREADY holds too strong to
    // hand on (#198, step 1). Same table, same split, same reason.
    const READER_ELEVATED_BITS = {{ enterprise_reader_elevated_bits | tojson }};
    // BigInt, not `&`. SP.BasePermissions.Low runs to 2^32-1 and JS bitwise
    // operators truncate to a SIGNED 32-bit integer, so the top bit of a
    // full mask reads negative. Every bit this file tests happens to sit low
    // enough for that to come out right, which is exactly the kind of
    // accident a later bit would break silently.
    const bitmapHalf = (perms, half, levelName) => {
      const raw = perms == null ? undefined : perms[half];
      const text = String(raw == null ? '' : raw);
      // Int64 under odata=verbose is a STRING; some responses give a number.
      // Anything else means the read did not return a bitmap, and guessing
      // zero would report every bit missing rather than an unreadable level.
      if (!/^\d+$/.test(text)) {
        throw new Error(
          `permission level '${levelName}' returned BasePermissions.${half} as `
          + `${JSON.stringify(raw)}, which is not a bitmap this script can read`,
        );
      }
      return BigInt(text);
    };
    const carriesBit = (high, low, bit) => (
      (high & BigInt(bit.high)) === BigInt(bit.high)
      && (low & BigInt(bit.low)) === BigInt(bit.low)
    );
    // A role definition's live bitmap, by Id. Step 0 resolves the levels this
    // bundle declares by NAME because that is what the mapping gives it; step
    // 1 has an Id off a binding and nothing else, and resolving that back
    // through a name would reintroduce exactly the name-is-not-the-grant
    // hazard step 0 exists to close.
    const readLevelById = async (levelId, boundName) => {
      const label = boundName || `role definition ${levelId}`;
      const resp = await fetchWithRetry(
        apiUrl(`web/roledefinitions(${levelId})?$select=Id,Name,BasePermissions`),
        { headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json;odata=verbose' } },
      );
      if (!resp.ok) {
        const text = await resp.text();
        throw new Error(
          `the permission level behind binding ${levelId} ('${label}') could not be read: `
          + `HTTP ${resp.status} ${text}. Nothing was changed.`,
        );
      }
      const def = (await resp.json()).d || {};
      const name = typeof def.Name === 'string' && def.Name !== '' ? def.Name : label;
      return {
        name,
        high: bitmapHalf(def.BasePermissions, 'High', name),
        low: bitmapHalf(def.BasePermissions, 'Low', name),
      };
    };
    for (const grp of readerGroups) {
      try {
        // 0. The GRANT, by its bitmap and not by its name. #199.
        //
        // Every step below guards WHO is enrolled. This one guards WHAT they
        // are enrolled into. A permission level is site-scoped and its name
        // is writable, so a level called 'Read' is evidence that somebody
        // named it that and nothing more. The build reserves the built-in
        // names against its OWN declarations (analysis/permissions.py,
        // BUILT_IN_LEVELS) and refuses a mapping that redefines one, which
        // says nothing about what the target site already has. Bind a level
        // called Read that grants nothing and the deploy reads back
        // byte-identical and reports success; the reporting account holds a
        // grant that reaches no rows and nobody finds out until somebody
        // opens the report.
        //
        // Runs BEFORE step 2, which is a write: a level this run is going to
        // refuse must not materialise a principal in the site's user
        // information list on the way to being refused.
        //
        // MEASURED against the real thing rather than assumed, per the
        // reference-implementation rule: `reader-bindings-probe.js` read the
        // built-in Read on a live site on 2026-08-14 (#199, R2) as
        // High=176 Low=138612833, which carries all three required bits and
        // both advisory ones. So a genuine built-in Read passes this check
        // silently, which is the property that makes a firing check mean
        // something.
        //
        // And the threat it guards against is measured too, not supposed.
        // `read-level-tamper-probe.js` ran on a live site on 2026-09-06 and
        // SharePoint Online ACCEPTED a MERGE of BasePermissions on the
        // BUILT-IN Read (RoleTypeKind 2, Id 1073741826): HTTP 204, reading
        // back High=176 Low=138612832 with ViewListItems gone. A tenant admin
        // or a rogue script can hollow out a built-in level in one call, and
        // the level goes on being called Read.
        //
        // The same day, a deploy against that tampered site refused here with
        // "permission level 'Read' on this site does not grant ViewListItems"
        // and aborted before list creation, issuing no POST, MERGE or DELETE
        // after this phase began. Restoring the bitmap by exact read-back and
        // rerunning enrolled the account. So this check is the only thing
        // between a hollowed-out level and a site that reports success.
        const levelNames = [...new Set(SCHEMA.list_assignments
          .flatMap(la => la.assignments)
          .filter(a => a.principal.kind === 'group' && a.principal.name === grp.name)
          .map(a => a.level))];
        // WARN rather than abort, and on a bundle this tool produced this is
        // now unreachable. The validator
        // (ENTERPRISE_READER_GROUP_NOT_GRANTED) requires the flagged group to
        // hold a grant SOMEWHERE in the mapping, and `list_assignments` holds
        // only the lists in the site role THIS bundle deploys, so a mapping
        // granting the reader in another role used to reach here with nothing
        // to check; `build` now refuses that combination outright (#200 gap
        // 3). It stays for a HAND-EDITED bundle, the same reason the loop
        // above is a loop, and stays a WARN because nothing is granted: there
        // is no bitmap that could be wrong and nothing to fail closed on.
        if (levelNames.length === 0) {
          log('WARN', `'${grp.name}' is granted no permission level on the lists this bundle `
            + `deploys, so enrolling '${READER_ADDRESS}' into it grants nothing here. Its `
            + `bitmap was not checked. If this family is meant to be readable, declare the `
            + `grant in list_permissions for this site role.`);
        }
        // The Ids step 0 judges, so step 1 does not report this bundle's own
        // declared grant back as an extra binding. Collected here rather than
        // re-resolved there: a redeploy holds the grant it declared, and
        // warning about it on every rerun trains an operator to ignore the
        // line that matters.
        const verifiedLevelIds = new Set();
        for (const levelName of levelNames) {
          const lvlResp = await fetchWithRetry(
            apiUrl(`web/roledefinitions/getbyname('${odataName(levelName)}')?$select=Id,Name,BasePermissions`),
            { headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json;odata=verbose' } },
          );
          if (!lvlResp.ok) {
            const text = await lvlResp.text();
            throw new Error(
              `permission level '${levelName}', which '${grp.name}' is granted, could not be read: `
              + `HTTP ${lvlResp.status} ${text}. Nothing was changed.`,
            );
          }
          const lvl = (await lvlResp.json()).d || {};
          const high = bitmapHalf(lvl.BasePermissions, 'High', levelName);
          const low = bitmapHalf(lvl.BasePermissions, 'Low', levelName);
          const missing = READER_REQUIRED_BITS.filter(b => !carriesBit(high, low, b));
          if (missing.length > 0) {
            throw new Error(
              `permission level '${levelName}' on this site does not grant `
              + `${missing.map(b => b.name).join(', ')}, so '${grp.name}' would not let `
              + `'${READER_ADDRESS}' read the lists this bundle deploys. Its bitmap is `
              + `High=${high} Low=${low}. A level's NAME is not its grant: this site's `
              + `'${levelName}' is not the built-in one, whatever it is called. Nothing was `
              + `granted and nobody was enrolled. Fix the level in Site settings > Site `
              + `permissions > Permission Levels, or grant the reader group a level that `
              + `does read, then run this script again.`,
            );
          }
          const advisory = READER_ADVISORY_BITS.filter(b => !carriesBit(high, low, b));
          if (advisory.length > 0) {
            // Not fatal: a browser-based reader works without either. A
            // reporting CLIENT does not, and this is the only place the
            // operator is told before the account is enrolled.
            log('WARN', `Permission level '${levelName}' grants `
              + `${advisory.map(b => b.name).join(' and ')} to nobody, so it is narrower than the `
              + `built-in Read. '${READER_ADDRESS}' will be able to read the lists in a browser. `
              + `A reporting client enumerating them over REST or CSOM needs UseRemoteAPIs, and `
              + `resolving person columns needs BrowseUserInfo. Enrolment continues.`);
          }
          if (lvl.Id != null) verifiedLevelIds.add(String(lvl.Id));
          log('INFO', `Permission level '${levelName}' carries the read bits `
            + `'${grp.name}' needs (High=${high} Low=${low}).`);
        }

        // 1. The group, and WHAT IT ALREADY HOLDS. #198.
        //
        // Step 0 judged the level this bundle grants. It cannot see a role
        // assignment the group carries for some other reason: an earlier
        // deploy, a hand edit, another tool. The group is resolved by NAME
        // and enrolled into, so the account inherits every one of those
        // bindings, permanently, and nothing here removes them:
        // `_acls.js.j2` reconciles the lists `SCHEMA.list_assignments` names
        // and nothing else. The level bitmap passes, the per-list grant
        // passes, and the drop-box posture leaks anyway.
        //
        // MEASURED, `test/manual/reader-bindings-probe.js` run 2,
        // 2026-08-14 (R4): on that site every tool-created group held
        // exactly `Limited Access` at web scope, the DERIVED binding
        // SharePoint adds when a principal is granted something on a list
        // below the web, and nothing elevated. So the reference posture
        // passes this silently, which is the property that makes a firing
        // check mean something.
        //
        // WEB SCOPE ONLY, and this is a boundary rather than an oversight.
        // R5 of the same run reports the other half, an elevated binding on
        // a LIST outside this bundle, and it is deliberately NOT checked
        // here: the shipped `deployment-log` family grants this same group
        // Full Control on its central log list on purpose, and says why
        // (`solutions/deployment-log/20-configure/mapping.yaml`). A
        // list-scope arm would refuse that posture on any site carrying both
        // families, which is a rule stronger than the reference
        // implementation satisfies. Closing it needs that decision revisited
        // first, not a wider scan bolted on here.
        //
        // Runs BEFORE step 2 for the reason step 0 does: EnsureUser is a
        // write, and a run that is going to refuse must not materialise a
        // principal in the site's user information list on the way to
        // refusing it. The group probe itself moved up from what used to be
        // step 5 so that its Id is available here; it is a GET either way,
        // against the group Phase {{ phase.group_number }}.2 created and
        // reconciled earlier in this same run.
        const grpResp = await fetchWithRetry(apiUrl(`web/sitegroups/getbyname('${odataName(grp.name)}')?$select=Id`), {
          headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json;odata=verbose' },
        });
        if (!grpResp.ok) {
          throw new Error(`group probe for '${grp.name}' failed: HTTP ${grpResp.status}`);
        }
        const groupId = (await grpResp.json()).d.Id;
        if (!Number.isInteger(groupId)) {
          throw new Error(`group probe for '${grp.name}' returned an invalid response`);
        }

        // The collection form, not `roleassignments/getbyprincipalid`, and
        // the url is `_security_principals.js.j2`'s verbatim. This is the
        // shape the probe actually measured at web scope (R4c: PASS as a
        // site owner on 2026-08-14, HTTP 403 as a Visitor on 2026-09-02),
        // and a 404-for-absence convention borrowed from list scope would be
        // an inference where a measurement exists.
        const heldBindings = new Map();
        {
          let asgUrl = apiUrl('web/roleassignments?$select=PrincipalId&$expand=RoleDefinitionBindings&$top=200');
          while (asgUrl) {
            const asgResp = await fetchWithRetry(asgUrl, {
              headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json;odata=verbose' },
            });
            if (!asgResp.ok) {
              const text = await asgResp.text();
              throw new Error(
                `the role assignments '${grp.name}' already holds on this site could not be `
                + `enumerated: HTTP ${asgResp.status} ${text}. That read is the only evidence `
                + `this run can have that the group carries nothing beyond the grant this bundle `
                + `declares, and a refusal reads exactly like a clean group. Nothing was granted `
                + `and nobody was enrolled. Run this script as a site owner.`,
              );
            }
            const asgJson = await asgResp.json();
            if (!asgJson || !asgJson.d || !Array.isArray(asgJson.d.results)) {
              throw new Error(
                `the role-assignment enumeration for '${grp.name}' returned an invalid response`,
              );
            }
            for (const row of asgJson.d.results) {
              if (Number(row.PrincipalId) !== Number(groupId)) continue;
              // Same tolerance, and same reason, as countWebAssignmentsUsing
              // in `_security_principals.js.j2`: how odata=verbose renders an
              // expanded navigation property is inferred there rather than
              // measured, so both shapes are accepted and NEITHER shape
              // throws. A row whose bindings cannot be read is a binding this
              // scan cannot see, and counting it as clean is the same false
              // reassurance as a refused enumeration.
              const raw = row.RoleDefinitionBindings;
              const bindings = Array.isArray(raw) ? raw : (raw && raw.results);
              if (!Array.isArray(bindings)) {
                throw new Error(
                  `a role assignment held by '${grp.name}' returned bindings this script cannot `
                  + `read, so its privileges cannot be judged. Nothing was changed.`,
                );
              }
              for (const b of bindings) {
                if (b == null || b.Id == null) {
                  throw new Error(
                    `a role assignment held by '${grp.name}' returned a binding with no Id`,
                  );
                }
                heldBindings.set(String(b.Id), typeof b.Name === 'string' ? b.Name : '');
              }
            }
            asgUrl = asgJson.d.__next || null;
          }
        }

        const elevated = [];
        const lesser = [];
        for (const [levelId, boundName] of heldBindings) {
          // Judged by BITMAP, always, and for every binding. The name only
          // ever chooses how loudly a binding already proved harmless is
          // reported (below); it never decides whether one is harmless. A
          // site can carry a level called 'Limited Access' that grants
          // Full Control, which is #199's lesson one scope up.
          if (verifiedLevelIds.has(levelId)) continue;
          const lvl = await readLevelById(levelId, boundName);
          const carried = READER_ELEVATED_BITS.filter(b => carriesBit(lvl.high, lvl.low, b));
          if (carried.length > 0) {
            elevated.push(`'${lvl.name}' (${carried.map(b => b.name).join(', ')})`);
          } else {
            lesser.push(lvl.name);
          }
        }
        if (elevated.length > 0) {
          throw new Error(
            `group '${grp.name}' already holds ${elevated.length} role assignment(s) on this `
            + `site that grant more than reading, which this bundle did not create: `
            + `${elevated.join('; ')}. Enrolling '${READER_ADDRESS}' into it would hand that `
            + `account every one of those rights, and no phase of this deploy removes them: the `
            + `ACL phase reconciles only the lists this bundle declares. Nothing was granted and `
            + `nobody was enrolled. Remove the binding in Site settings > Site permissions, or `
            + `point --enterprise-reader at a group that holds nothing else, then run this `
            + `script again.`,
          );
        }
        for (const name of lesser) {
          // Visible, not blocking. A level no stronger than the declared
          // grant hands the account nothing it does not already have, so
          // refusing would fail a posture that is untidy rather than unsafe.
          // 'Limited Access' is the DERIVED binding every tool-created group
          // on the measured site carries, so it is reported at INFO: warning
          // about it on every redeploy is how a warning stops being read.
          const line = `Group '${grp.name}' already holds '${name}' at web scope, which `
            + `'${READER_ADDRESS}' inherits on enrolment. It carries no permission beyond `
            + `reading, so the enrolment continues.`;
          if (name === 'Limited Access') {
            log('INFO', `Group '${grp.name}' also holds '${name}' at web scope, the binding `
              + `SharePoint derives from a grant below the web. It carries no permission beyond `
              + `reading.`);
          } else {
            log('WARN', line);
          }
        }
        log('INFO', `'${grp.name}' holds ${heldBindings.size} web-scope binding(s), none of `
          + `them elevated.`);

        // 2. Resolve the address. EnsureUser is a write (it materialises
        // the principal in the site's user information list), hence the
        // digest, and it is the only documented way to turn a UPN into the
        // claims LoginName a group membership needs.
        const digestR = await getDigest();
        const resolveResp = await fetchWithRetry(apiUrl('web/ensureuser?$select=Id,LoginName,Title,Email,PrincipalType'), {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: spHeaders(digestR),
          body: JSON.stringify({ logonName: READER_ADDRESS }),
        });
        if (!resolveResp.ok) {
          const text = await resolveResp.text();
          throw new Error(`could not resolve '${READER_ADDRESS}': HTTP ${resolveResp.status} ${text}`);
        }
        const resolved = (await resolveResp.json()).d;
        if (!resolved || !Number.isInteger(resolved.Id)
            || typeof resolved.LoginName !== 'string' || resolved.LoginName === '') {
          throw new Error(`resolving '${READER_ADDRESS}' returned an invalid principal response`);
        }

        // 3. Principal type, STRICT equality. Microsoft Learn documents
        // SP.PrincipalType as None 0, User 1, DistributionList 2,
        // SecurityGroup 4, SharePointGroup 8, All 15, and marks it
        // [Flags]. A bitwise `& 1` would therefore admit any combination
        // that happens to carry the User bit, so the comparison is `!== 1`
        // and stays that way.
        if (resolved.PrincipalType !== 1) {
          throw new Error(
            `'${READER_ADDRESS}' resolved to '${resolved.Title || resolved.LoginName}' with PrincipalType `
            + `${resolved.PrincipalType}, which is not a single user (1). Enrolling a group would grant Read `
            + `to every one of its members. Nothing was changed; name an individual account.`,
          );
        }

        // 4. The tenant-wide claims. Granting one hands Read on this
        // register to every account in the tenant, the one mistake in this
        // phase with no cheap undo.
        //
        // MEASURED 2026-08-12, on ONE tenant, by group B of
        // `test/manual/enterprise-reader-probe.js`: `web/ensureuser` for
        // BOTH display names ("Everyone" and "Everyone except external
        // users") resolved to the SAME principal, a
        // `c:0-.f|rolemanager|spo-grid-all-users/<tenantid>` login typed
        // PrincipalType 4 (SecurityGroup). So the strict `!== 1` check
        // above refuses them on its own, before this list is consulted.
        // An earlier version of this comment said these claims "resolve as
        // ordinary users, so the type check above does not catch them".
        // That was written from plausibility and the live run contradicts
        // it.
        //
        // THE NEEDLES STAY, and neither they nor the type check are to be
        // softened. Two of the three matched that principal
        // (`spo-grid-all-users`, `c:0-.f|rolemanager`), and ONE TENANT IS
        // ONE DATA POINT: another tenant may type the same claim
        // differently, and a claim typed 1 with no needle matching it is
        // exactly the case this defence in depth exists for.
        //
        // KNOWN LIMIT, opened 2026-08-11, NARROWED 2026-08-12. Learn
        // ("Grant the Everyone claim to external users in Microsoft 365")
        // names FOUR tenant-wide claims: Everyone, Everyone except external
        // users, All Authenticated Users, All Forms Users. The three
        // needles below cover the first two. Learn publishes no login-name
        // encoding for the other two, and they are still deliberately NOT
        // guessed here: a needle invented from plausibility would read
        // like a guard and catch nothing, which is worse than an
        // acknowledged gap.
        //
        // They were SOUGHT on 2026-08-12: that probe's
        // `access.principal.ensureuser-all-authenticated-users` and
        // `access.principal.ensureuser-all-forms-users` rows (B3 and B4)
        // asked `web/ensureuser` for both display names and it REFUSED
        // both, HTTP 400 SPException "The specified user ... could not be
        // found", under odata=nometadata and odata=verbose alike. So on
        // that tenant neither is reachable by display name, and
        // `validate_enterprise_reader` refuses any '|' at build time, so a
        // claim string cannot be supplied through the CLI either.
        //
        // THAT NARROWS THE GAP; IT DOES NOT CLOSE IT. Another tenant may
        // resolve those display names, and a refusal by display name is not
        // proof that no login-name encoding exists.
        //
        // The residual risk therefore still rests on the guards rather than
        // on this list: the '|' refusal at build time; the strict type
        // check above; and step 5 below, which requires the RESOLVED
        // identity to equal the address that was asked for, which a claim
        // principal does not.
        const login = resolved.LoginName.toLowerCase();
        const claim = ['spo-grid-all-users', 'c:0(.s|true', 'c:0-.f|rolemanager']
          .find(c => login.includes(c));
        if (claim) {
          throw new Error(
            `'${READER_ADDRESS}' resolved to '${resolved.LoginName}', which is a tenant-wide claim `
            + `(matched '${claim}') and would grant Read to every user in the tenant. Nothing was changed.`,
          );
        }

        // 5. Identity. EnsureUser answering with SOMEBODY ELSE is the quiet
        // failure here: the deploy reports success and the wrong account
        // holds Read. Two addresses are compared, and either may match: the
        // mailbox Email, and the UPN the claims LoginName ends in. They are
        // routinely different for the same account (an alias mailbox), so
        // requiring the Email alone would refuse correct resolutions, while
        // a resolution of a DIFFERENT account matches neither.
        const upnPart = (name) => {
          const parts = String(name).split('|');
          return parts[parts.length - 1];
        };
        const candidates = [resolved.Email, upnPart(resolved.LoginName)]
          .map(v => String(v == null ? '' : v).toLowerCase())
          .filter(v => v !== '');
        if (!candidates.includes(READER_ADDRESS.toLowerCase())) {
          throw new Error(
            `'${READER_ADDRESS}' resolved to a different account: '${resolved.Title || resolved.LoginName}' `
            + `(${candidates.join(', ') || 'no address'}). Nothing was changed.`,
          );
        }

        // 6. Membership, enumerated in full (every page), because the gate
        // at step 7 is only as good as the weakest page of the read: a
        // stranger on page two that this loop never fetched would read as
        // an empty group. The same read verifies the write below.
        const readMembers = async () => {
          const found = [];
          let membersUrl = apiUrl(`web/sitegroups(${groupId})/users?$select=Id,LoginName,Title&$top=5000`);
          while (membersUrl) {
            const membersResp = await fetchWithRetry(membersUrl, {
              headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json;odata=verbose' },
            });
            if (!membersResp.ok) {
              const text = await membersResp.text();
              throw new Error(`membership probe for '${grp.name}' failed: HTTP ${membersResp.status} ${text}`);
            }
            const membersJson = await membersResp.json();
            if (!membersJson.d || !Array.isArray(membersJson.d.results)) {
              throw new Error(`membership probe for '${grp.name}' returned an invalid response`);
            }
            found.push(...membersJson.d.results);
            membersUrl = membersJson.d.__next || null;
          }
          return found;
        };
        // nameKey: claims logins are resolved case-insensitively, so a
        // member stored as I:0#.F|... must not read as a different account.
        const isReader = (u) => nameKey(u && u.LoginName) === nameKey(resolved.LoginName);
        const before = await readMembers();

        // 7. Anybody else in the group ABORTS the phase, and does so BEFORE
        // anything is added. Two rules meet here and neither bends:
        //
        // - Nobody is ever removed. Membership is an operator-owned concern
        //   and this is a gate, not a reconciler.
        // - The operator must be shown a state this run did not just change,
        //   so the abort comes before the enrolment POST, not after it.
        //
        // This used to be an INFO line and the run carried on. That made a
        // mistyped-but-valid address permanent: enrol it, notice, redeploy
        // with the right address, and BOTH accounts hold Read on every list
        // this bundle provisions, for as long as the group exists. The only
        // record was one INFO line in a run that reported success, which is
        // to say no record at all.
        //
        // The named account already being a member is NOT this case (a
        // redeploy with the same flag has to stay green), so it is filtered
        // out by isReader before the count is taken.
        // ONE spelling of the invariant, because it is checked twice: here
        // against the state this run found, and again against the state it
        // left. Two hand-written copies would be two things to keep in step.
        const foreign = members => members.filter(u => !isReader(u));
        const listMembers = members => members
          .map(u => `'${u.Title || '(untitled)'}' (${u.LoginName})`)
          .join('; ');
        // The same invariant applied to the state this run LEAVES, which is
        // asked on BOTH ways out of this block: the enrolment below, and the
        // already-a-member path that adds nothing. `before` is a snapshot,
        // and the run does not end at it -- lists are created for as long as
        // the rest of the deploy takes. Checking only the writing path left
        // the other one reporting success against a snapshot taken before
        // the work started, on a group whose entire purpose is that it holds
        // one account.
        //
        // Nothing removes a STRANGER here, for the reason the before-read
        // gives: membership is an operator-owned concern and this is a gate,
        // not a reconciler for accounts this run did not add. It fails closed
        // AFTER the read, which is deliberate -- the operator is told exactly
        // what the group now holds rather than left to find out. What happens
        // to THIS run's own write differs between the two callers, so `undo`
        // is the caller's sentence to supply.
        const refuseLateArrivals = (after, undo) => {
          const strays = foreign(after);
          if (strays.length === 0) return;
          throw new Error(
            `group '${grp.name}' now also holds ${strays.length} principal(s) that were not `
            + `there when this run checked: ${listMembers(strays)}. Somebody added them while `
            + `this script was running. That group grants Read on EVERY list this bundle `
            + `provisions, so each of them can read all of it. ${undo} Review the membership `
            + `in Site permissions > Groups for the others and remove whoever should not be `
            + `there, then run this script again.`,
          );
        };
        const others = foreign(before);
        if (others.length > 0) {
          const listed = listMembers(others);
          throw new Error(
            `group '${grp.name}' already holds ${others.length} principal(s) other than `
            + `'${READER_ADDRESS}': ${listed}. This group is shared by every family deployed `
            + `to the site, so an unexpected member is most likely another family's enterprise `
            + `reader rather than a mistake; removing it by hand would silently break that `
            + `family's reporting. Nothing was granted and nobody was removed. This needs a `
            + `human decision. The site must agree on one reader account: either remove them in `
            + `Site permissions > Groups only after confirming no other family still needs them, `
            + `or rebuild WITHOUT --enterprise-reader, which leaves the membership exactly as it `
            + `is and still deploys the group and its declared Read grant.`,
          );
        }
        if (before.some(isReader)) {
          // A redeploy writes nothing here, and used to `continue` on the
          // strength of `before` alone. That made the whole gate a check on
          // one snapshot: a principal added between this read and the end of
          // the run passed unnoticed, and the run reported success on a group
          // holding a second reader. The window is the length of the deploy,
          // not the length of this phase, and a deploy is pasted into a site
          // while people are working in it.
          refuseLateArrivals(
            await readMembers(),
            `This run enrolled nobody -- '${READER_ADDRESS}' was already a member -- so it `
            + `has no write of its own to undo.`,
          );
          log('INFO', `'${resolved.Title || READER_ADDRESS}' is already a member of '${grp.name}'; membership left untouched.`);
          continue;
        }

        const digestA = await getDigest();
        const addResp = await fetchWithRetry(apiUrl(`web/sitegroups(${groupId})/users`), {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: spHeaders(digestA),
          body: JSON.stringify({ __metadata: { type: 'SP.User' }, LoginName: resolved.LoginName }),
        });
        if (!addResp.ok) {
          const text = await addResp.text();
          throw new Error(`enrolment of '${READER_ADDRESS}' into '${grp.name}' failed: HTTP ${addResp.status} ${text}`);
        }

        // Recorded now, before the read-back below can abort this phase.
        // Two concurrent deploys with different addresses can each reach
        // this point, each add their own account, and each then abort at
        // the strays check a few lines down because it now sees the
        // other's account too (#213 form 1). Recording only on full
        // success left an aborting run with nothing to undo, which was the
        // issue. Mirrors _operator_enrolment.js.j2's selfEnrollments.push:
        // right after the write that needs undoing, not after the checks
        // that follow it.
        readerEnrollments.push({ groupId, groupName: grp.name, userId: resolved.Id });

        // 8. Read the membership back. HTTP 200 is not evidence that the
        // account is in the group, and a grant reported but never made is
        // worse than a failure: the operator stops looking.
        const after = await readMembers();
        if (!after.some(isReader)) {
          throw new Error(`'${READER_ADDRESS}' was accepted into '${grp.name}' but is absent when the membership is read back; the grant did not take effect`);
        }
        // The same invariant the before-read enforced, applied to the state
        // this run LEFT. This run's OWN account is not a stray: it was
        // recorded above and deploy.js.j2's finally removes it, because the
        // abort below means the run never reached the end.
        refuseLateArrivals(
          after,
          `This run's own enrolment of '${READER_ADDRESS}' will be removed automatically as `
          + `this script exits, since the run does not reach its end.`,
        );
        log('INFO', `Enrolled '${resolved.Title || READER_ADDRESS}' into '${grp.name}' with the group's read-only grants. This membership is PERMANENT once this run reaches its end; if a later phase aborts it is removed automatically. Once permanent, remove it in Site permissions > Groups when the account no longer needs access.`);
        logChange({ key: `reader: ${READER_ADDRESS}`, kind: 'reader-enrolled',
          target: READER_ADDRESS, oldValue: '',
          newValue: `enrolled in '${grp.name}' by deploy ${RELEASE_TAG}` });
      } catch (err) {
        log('ERROR', `Enterprise reader enrolment for '${grp.name}': ${err.message}`);
        summary.errors.push({ phase: '{{ phase.number }}', group: grp.name, error: err.message });
        readerEnrolmentErrors += 1;
      }
    }
  }
  // THIS phase's errors, not the run's. `summary.errors` accumulates across
  // every phase, so reading the whole array would claim an earlier phase's
  // failure as a reader-enrolment one -- logging 'Enterprise reader
  // enrolment failed' and returning `aborted: 'reader-enrolment-errors'` for
  // something this phase never touched.
  //
  // LATENT, NOT LIVE, and the distinction is worth writing down. TRACED
  // 2026-08-12: every PREPARE phase ahead of this one -- preflight,
  // security, operator enrolment -- ends with the same whole-array check and
  // RETURNS. So an earlier error aborts at its own phase and this array is
  // necessarily empty by the time control reaches here. A reviewer reported
  // the misattribution and the reasoning was sound in the abstract; the
  // chain is what makes it unreachable today.
  //
  // Fixed anyway, because "correct only because of what four other files
  // happen to do" is not a property this phase can see or keep. The day one
  // of those phases records a non-fatal error, this abort starts lying.
  //
  // Counted locally rather than filtered on the `phase` key, because a count
  // needs no agreement with how errors are SHAPED elsewhere -- that key is
  // set by the push above, and a change to it would quietly widen this check
  // again. Untested for the same reason it is latent: the state cannot be
  // reached without hand-editing the emitted script, and a test that
  // fabricates it would be asserting about a scenario, not a behaviour.
  if (readerEnrolmentErrors > 0) {
    log('ERROR', 'Enterprise reader enrolment failed; aborting before list creation.');
    return { ...summary, aborted: 'reader-enrolment-errors' };
  }
{% endif %}
